{
  "set1": [
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon FSx for Lustre",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A research company runs genomics workloads on a fleet of several hundred Amazon EC2 instances. Every job reads the same 200 TB reference dataset that is stored in Amazon S3. The jobs require a shared POSIX file system that delivers sub-millisecond latency and hundreds of GB/s of aggregate throughput. Results written by the jobs must be exported back to the S3 bucket automatically. Which storage solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an Amazon FSx for Lustre file system with a data repository association to the S3 bucket and mount it on all EC2 instances.",
        "B": "Create an Amazon EFS file system in General Purpose performance mode with Elastic throughput and copy the dataset into it with AWS DataSync.",
        "C": "Attach a single Amazon EBS io2 Block Express volume with Multi-Attach enabled to all of the EC2 instances.",
        "D": "Deploy an Amazon S3 File Gateway on an EC2 instance and mount the NFS share on all of the compute instances."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Parallel file system for HPC:** FSx for Lustre is a high-performance parallel file system built for HPC, ML, and genomics workloads, providing sub-millisecond latencies and up to hundreds of GB/s of throughput and millions of IOPS.\n* **Native S3 integration:** A data repository association (DRA) links the file system to an S3 bucket. Objects are lazy-loaded into the file system on first access, and automatic export can write new and changed files back to S3.\n* **Shared POSIX access:** Hundreds or thousands of Linux clients can mount the same file system concurrently.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** EFS is a scalable shared NFS file system, but it is not a parallel file system and does not deliver the hundreds of GB/s, sub-millisecond performance profile of Lustre. It also has no native linkage that exports results back to S3.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** EBS Multi-Attach works only with io1/io2 volumes, attaches to at most 16 Nitro instances in the same Availability Zone, and requires a cluster-aware file system. It cannot serve several hundred instances.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** S3 File Gateway is a hybrid service that presents S3 over NFS/SMB through a single gateway appliance with a local cache. That single gateway becomes a throughput bottleneck and cannot provide HPC-class performance.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **HPC / ML / genomics + S3 dataset + high throughput:** Think Amazon FSx for Lustre with an S3 data repository association.\n2. **EFS vs. FSx for Lustre:** EFS = general-purpose shared Linux file storage that scales automatically; FSx for Lustre = parallel, compute-intensive workloads that need extreme throughput."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EBS",
        "Amazon EC2"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a self-managed, latency-sensitive OLTP database on a single Nitro-based Amazon EC2 instance. Load testing shows the database needs a single block storage volume that sustains 180,000 IOPS with sub-millisecond latency. The data must persist if the instance is stopped and must be stored on highly durable storage. Which storage configuration meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Provision an Amazon EBS gp3 volume and configure it with the maximum IOPS and throughput settings.",
        "B": "Provision an Amazon EBS io2 Block Express volume with 180,000 provisioned IOPS.",
        "C": "Provision an Amazon EBS st1 Throughput Optimized HDD volume sized for the required throughput.",
        "D": "Store the database files on an Amazon EFS file system that uses Provisioned throughput mode."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Highest-performance EBS volume:** io2 Block Express supports up to 256,000 IOPS, 4,000 MiB/s, and 64 TiB per volume with sub-millisecond latency, so 180,000 IOPS on one volume is achievable.\n* **Durability:** io2 volumes are designed for 99.999% durability, higher than other EBS volume types, which suits mission-critical databases.\n* **Persistence:** EBS volumes persist independently of the instance lifecycle, unlike instance store.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** gp3 is the cost-effective default SSD volume and lets you provision IOPS independently of size, but it supports at most 80,000 IOPS and 2,000 MiB/s per volume, well below 180,000 IOPS, so it cannot meet the requirement on a single volume.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** st1 is an HDD volume optimized for large sequential throughput (for example, log processing). It cannot deliver high random IOPS or sub-millisecond latency and cannot be used for transactional database workloads like this.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** EFS is network file storage with higher per-operation latency than block storage. It is not designed to host a latency-sensitive OLTP database that needs sub-millisecond block I/O.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Very high IOPS + durability on one volume:** Choose EBS io2 Block Express (Nitro instances).\n2. **Default general-purpose SSD:** Choose gp3 over gp2: baseline 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s included, with IOPS and throughput provisioned independently of volume size.\n3. **HDD volumes:** st1/sc1 are for sequential throughput and cold data; they are never the answer for IOPS-heavy databases."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon S3",
        "S3 Transfer Acceleration",
        "S3 multipart upload"
      ],
      "prompt": "A media company stores user-submitted video files in an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-east-1 Region. Users in Asia and Europe upload files that are 2-8 GB in size and report that uploads are slow and often fail partway through, forcing them to restart from the beginning. The company wants to improve upload speed and reliability without deploying additional infrastructure in other Regions. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Change the default storage class of uploaded objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering.",
        "B": "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and have clients use the accelerate endpoint.",
        "C": "Spread the uploaded objects across a larger number of key prefixes in the bucket.",
        "D": "Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to replicate objects to buckets in Regions closer to the users.",
        "E": "Update the client application to use S3 multipart upload and upload parts in parallel."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B",
        "E"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options B & E are Correct\n\n* **Transfer Acceleration:** Routes uploads through the nearest CloudFront edge location and then over the optimized AWS backbone network to the bucket, which significantly improves long-distance transfer speed without deploying regional infrastructure.\n* **Multipart upload:** Splits large objects into parts that can be uploaded in parallel to increase throughput. If a part fails, only that part is retried instead of the entire file, which improves reliability. AWS recommends multipart upload for objects larger than 100 MB.\n* **Combined effect:** Together they address both the latency of long-distance uploads and the restart-from-scratch failure behavior.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Storage classes affect storage cost and retrieval characteristics. Intelligent-Tiering does not change upload speed or reliability.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** S3 supports at least 3,500 PUT requests per second per prefix. Adding prefixes helps with very high request rates, not with the speed of individual large uploads over long distances.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Cross-Region Replication copies objects after they have been written to the source bucket. It does not make the initial upload from Asia or Europe to us-east-1 any faster, and it effectively deploys buckets in other Regions.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Long-distance uploads to a single bucket:** S3 Transfer Acceleration.\n2. **Large files / unreliable networks:** Multipart upload with parallel parts; retry only failed parts.\n3. **Prefixes:** Prefix design matters for request-rate scaling, not single-object throughput."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EC2",
        "Cluster placement groups",
        "Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA)"
      ],
      "prompt": "A research institute runs a tightly coupled computational fluid dynamics simulation that uses MPI across 64 Amazon EC2 instances. Nodes exchange data constantly, and job runtime is dominated by inter-node network latency. The simulation can be rerun if it fails, so high availability across Availability Zones is not required. Which deployment will provide the BEST network performance between the nodes?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Launch the instances in a spread placement group across multiple Availability Zones.",
        "B": "Launch the instances in a partition placement group with each partition on separate racks.",
        "C": "Launch HPC-optimized instances that support Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) into a cluster placement group in a single Availability Zone, with EFA enabled.",
        "D": "Launch the instances in an Auto Scaling group across three Availability Zones behind a Network Load Balancer."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Cluster placement group:** Packs instances close together inside one Availability Zone, giving the low-latency, high-bandwidth node-to-node networking that tightly coupled workloads need.\n* **Elastic Fabric Adapter:** EFA is a network interface for EC2 that lets MPI and NCCL applications bypass the operating system kernel (OS-bypass) for lower and more consistent latency than TCP. Its enhanced networking builds on ENA.\n* **Trade-off accepted:** A single-AZ placement reduces fault isolation, which is acceptable because the job can be rerun.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Spread placement groups put each instance on distinct hardware to reduce correlated failures. They allow at most 7 running instances per AZ per group and do not optimize inter-node latency. Spanning AZs also adds latency.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Partition placement groups isolate groups of instances on separate racks for large distributed workloads such as HDFS, HBase, and Cassandra. They are built for fault isolation, not the lowest latency between all nodes.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Spreading nodes across AZs increases inter-node latency. A load balancer distributes client requests and plays no part in MPI node-to-node communication.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Tightly coupled HPC / MPI / low latency between nodes:** Cluster placement group + EFA (+ ENA enhanced networking).\n2. **Placement group types:** Cluster = performance (single AZ); partition = rack-level fault isolation for distributed data stores; spread = maximum isolation for a small number of critical instances."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Lambda"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company has an AWS Lambda function that resizes and watermarks images uploaded to Amazon S3. The function is configured with 128 MB of memory and uses about 95 MB at peak. CloudWatch metrics show that invocations take 40-50 seconds and are CPU-bound. The company wants to reduce processing time for each image. What should a solutions architect do?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Configure provisioned concurrency for the function.",
        "B": "Configure reserved concurrency for the function.",
        "C": "Increase the function timeout to 5 minutes.",
        "D": "Increase the memory allocated to the function."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Memory controls CPU:** Lambda allocates CPU power in proportion to configured memory. At 1,769 MB a function receives the equivalent of one full vCPU, and up to 6 vCPUs are available at 10,240 MB.\n* **CPU-bound workload:** Because the function is CPU-bound, more memory (and therefore more CPU) directly shortens execution time, often with little or no increase in total cost because duration drops.\n* **Right-sizing:** Tools such as AWS Compute Optimizer or Lambda Power Tuning can identify the optimal memory setting.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Provisioned concurrency keeps execution environments initialized to remove cold-start latency. It does not make each invocation's CPU-bound processing faster.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Reserved concurrency guarantees and caps the number of concurrent executions. It has no effect on per-invocation performance.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** A longer timeout only allows slow invocations to run longer; it does not reduce processing time.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Lambda is slow and CPU-bound:** Increase memory to get more vCPU.\n2. **Cold starts:** Provisioned concurrency or (for supported runtimes) SnapStart.\n3. **Concurrency limits / protect downstream:** Reserved concurrency."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Batch",
        "Amazon EC2 Spot Instances",
        "Amazon ECS"
      ],
      "prompt": "An engineering firm runs thousands of containerized simulation jobs each night. Each job runs for 1-3 hours, some jobs depend on the output of earlier jobs, and the jobs can tolerate interruption and be retried. The firm wants to use Spot capacity to reduce cost and does not want to install or manage a job scheduler or compute cluster. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Use AWS Batch with a managed compute environment that uses Spot Instances, define job dependencies, and configure automatic retries.",
        "B": "Package each simulation as an AWS Lambda function and invoke the functions from an Amazon EventBridge Scheduler schedule.",
        "C": "Launch an Amazon EMR cluster with Spot task nodes and submit each simulation as an EMR step.",
        "D": "Deploy an AWS Elastic Beanstalk worker environment that reads simulation jobs from an Amazon SQS queue."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Purpose-built batch service:** AWS Batch provisions and scales compute (EC2, Spot, or Fargate) based on queued jobs and handles queuing and scheduling, so no scheduler or cluster has to be managed.\n* **Job dependencies and retries:** Batch supports job dependencies (a job starts after others succeed) and retry strategies, which suits interruption-tolerant Spot workloads.\n* **Containers:** Batch jobs are defined as container images, matching the existing packaging.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Lambda has a maximum execution time of 15 minutes, so 1-3 hour simulations cannot run on it.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Amazon EMR is designed for big data frameworks such as Spark and Hive. Running generic containerized simulations with dependencies as EMR steps is a poor fit and still requires cluster management.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** An Elastic Beanstalk worker environment processes SQS messages but has no native job-dependency handling or Spot-aware batch scheduling, and the long-running jobs would need custom orchestration.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Batch jobs, job queues, dependencies, Spot, no scheduler to manage:** AWS Batch.\n2. **Over 15 minutes of runtime:** Rules out Lambda; consider Batch, ECS/Fargate, or EC2.\n3. **Spark/Hadoop big data processing:** Amazon EMR."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Aurora",
        "Aurora Replicas"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs an order-management application on an Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible DB cluster that has a single writer instance. A new set of business-intelligence dashboards runs heavy read-only queries throughout the day, and write latency for the application has increased significantly. The dashboards can tolerate replication lag of a few seconds. Which solution will improve application performance with the LEAST operational overhead?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Switch the cluster storage configuration to Aurora I/O-Optimized.",
        "B": "Add Aurora Replicas to the cluster and point the dashboards to the cluster reader endpoint.",
        "C": "Enable Aurora Backtrack on the cluster so that dashboards can query earlier points in time.",
        "D": "Export the data nightly to Amazon S3 and have the dashboards query the exports with Amazon Athena."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Offload reads:** Aurora Replicas share the cluster's storage volume and can serve read traffic, removing the reporting load from the writer instance.\n* **Reader endpoint:** The reader endpoint load-balances connections across all available Aurora Replicas, so adding replicas later requires no application change.\n* **Low lag:** Aurora Replicas typically lag the writer by less than 100 milliseconds, well within the dashboards' tolerance.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Aurora I/O-Optimized is a pricing configuration that removes per-I/O charges for I/O-heavy workloads. It does not offload read queries from the writer.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Backtrack rewinds the cluster to an earlier point in time for recovery. It is not a read-scaling feature and would affect the production cluster.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Nightly exports mean the dashboards would see data up to a day old, which violates the few-seconds-of-lag requirement, and building the export pipeline adds operational work.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Read-heavy workload slowing the writer:** Add read replicas (Aurora Replicas, up to 15) and use the reader endpoint.\n2. **Aurora endpoints:** Cluster (writer) endpoint for writes, reader endpoint for load-balanced reads, custom endpoints for subsets of instances."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Redshift",
        "Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL"
      ],
      "prompt": "A retail company runs its order-processing application on Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Analysts also run complex SQL aggregations and joins over 5 years of historical sales data, which is now 40 TB and growing quickly. These analytical queries take hours, slow down the transactional workload, and use BI tools that connect through standard SQL drivers. The company wants fast analytical queries that scale with data growth without managing database infrastructure. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Migrate the historical sales data to Amazon DynamoDB and create global secondary indexes for each analytical query.",
        "B": "Move the RDS DB instance to the largest available instance class with io2 Provisioned IOPS storage.",
        "C": "Load the historical sales data into Amazon Redshift Serverless and point the BI tools at the Redshift endpoint.",
        "D": "Migrate the historical sales data to Amazon Neptune and run the analytical queries as graph traversals."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Purpose-built data warehouse:** Amazon Redshift uses columnar storage, data compression, and massively parallel processing (MPP), so complex aggregations and joins over terabytes to petabytes of data run far faster than on a row-oriented OLTP database.\n* **Workload isolation:** Moving analytics to Redshift removes the heavy queries from the RDS instance, so order processing is no longer slowed down.\n* **Serverless and SQL-compatible:** Redshift Serverless provisions and scales capacity automatically, and BI tools connect through standard JDBC/ODBC drivers.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** DynamoDB is a key-value and document database optimized for known access patterns. It does not support ad hoc SQL joins and aggregations, and a GSI per query is impractical.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** A bigger OLTP instance is a temporary fix that has a hard ceiling. A row-store database still scans entire rows for analytical queries, and analytics would still compete with transactions on the same instance.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Neptune is a graph database for highly connected data such as social networks or fraud rings. It is not designed for SQL-based aggregations over large sales datasets.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **OLTP vs. OLAP:** RDS/Aurora for transactions; Amazon Redshift for complex analytical SQL over large historical datasets.\n2. **Columnar + MPP + BI tools:** Amazon Redshift (Serverless for no capacity management).\n3. **Ad hoc SQL on data already in S3:** Amazon Athena or Redshift Spectrum."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon DynamoDB",
        "DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)"
      ],
      "prompt": "A retail company uses an Amazon DynamoDB table to store product availability for its mobile app. The workload is read-heavy, the same items are read repeatedly, and the business now requires microsecond read latency for these hot items. Developers want to keep using the existing DynamoDB API calls with minimal code changes and do not want to implement cache-invalidation logic. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy an Amazon ElastiCache (Valkey) cluster and implement a cache-aside pattern in the application.",
        "B": "Deploy a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster and point the application's DynamoDB client to the DAX endpoint.",
        "C": "Switch the table from provisioned capacity mode to on-demand capacity mode.",
        "D": "Convert the table to a global table with replicas in additional AWS Regions."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **In-memory cache for DynamoDB:** DAX is a fully managed, write-through in-memory cache purpose-built for DynamoDB that reduces read latency from milliseconds to microseconds.\n* **API compatible:** DAX is API-compatible with DynamoDB. The application swaps to the DAX client and endpoint without rewriting its data access logic.\n* **No custom invalidation:** Writes go through DAX to DynamoDB, keeping the item cache consistent without application-managed invalidation.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** ElastiCache can deliver very low latency, but a cache-aside pattern requires new application code for reading, populating, and invalidating cache entries, which the developers want to avoid.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** On-demand mode removes capacity planning and absorbs traffic spikes, but it does not reduce DynamoDB's single-digit-millisecond latency to microseconds.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Global tables replicate data across Regions for multi-Region availability and local reads. They do not provide microsecond latency.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **DynamoDB + microsecond reads + minimal code change:** DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX).\n2. **Caching for relational databases or generic data:** Amazon ElastiCache (Valkey, Redis OSS, Memcached), often with a cache-aside or write-through strategy."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Global Accelerator",
        "Network Load Balancer"
      ],
      "prompt": "A gaming company hosts a multiplayer game backend that uses a custom UDP protocol. The backend runs behind Network Load Balancers in three AWS Regions. Players worldwide experience inconsistent latency, and the company's partners need a small, fixed set of IP addresses to allowlist. If a Region becomes unhealthy, traffic must be redirected to another Region within seconds, without relying on DNS caching behavior. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the Network Load Balancers as origins.",
        "B": "Create Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing records with health checks for each Network Load Balancer.",
        "C": "Consolidate the backend into a single Region behind one Network Load Balancer with Elastic IP addresses.",
        "D": "Create an AWS Global Accelerator standard accelerator with an endpoint group in each Region that contains that Region's Network Load Balancer."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Static anycast IPs:** Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IPv4 addresses that front endpoints in multiple Regions, giving partners a fixed allowlist.\n* **Performance:** Player traffic enters the AWS global network at the nearest edge location and travels on the AWS backbone, which reduces latency and jitter. TCP and UDP are both supported.\n* **Fast failover:** Global Accelerator continuously health-checks endpoints and shifts traffic to healthy endpoint groups within seconds, independent of DNS TTLs and client caching.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** CloudFront is a content delivery network for HTTP/HTTPS (and WebSocket) traffic. It does not proxy custom UDP protocols.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Route 53 latency routing returns different IP addresses per Region, so there is no small fixed set to allowlist, and failover depends on DNS TTLs and client resolver caching.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** A single Region increases latency for distant players and eliminates multi-Region failover.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **UDP / non-HTTP + static IPs + global + fast regional failover:** AWS Global Accelerator.\n2. **Cacheable HTTP content at the edge:** Amazon CloudFront.\n3. **DNS-based routing:** Route 53 policies are subject to TTL and caching delays."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Transit Gateway",
        "AWS Direct Connect",
        "AWS RAM"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company has 60 VPCs across multiple AWS accounts in one Region and expects to add more each quarter. Workloads in most VPCs must communicate with each other and with the on-premises data center, which is connected to AWS through AWS Direct Connect. The current design of VPC peering connections and per-VPC VPN tunnels has become difficult to manage. Which network architecture will scale with the LEAST operational overhead?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy an AWS Transit Gateway, share it with the other accounts by using AWS RAM, attach all VPCs, and connect it to the data center through a Direct Connect gateway with a transit virtual interface.",
        "B": "Create a full mesh of VPC peering connections between all VPCs and a separate AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection from each VPC to the data center.",
        "C": "Build a transit VPC with third-party VPN appliances on Amazon EC2 instances and connect every VPC to it with VPN tunnels.",
        "D": "Create AWS PrivateLink endpoint services in each VPC and interface endpoints in every other VPC that needs access."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Hub-and-spoke:** Transit Gateway acts as a regional cloud router. Each VPC needs only one attachment, and route tables control which attachments can communicate, scaling to thousands of attachments.\n* **Multi-account:** AWS RAM shares the transit gateway across accounts (or the whole organization) so other accounts can attach their VPCs.\n* **Hybrid:** A Direct Connect gateway associated with the transit gateway over a transit VIF gives all attached VPCs access to on-premises networks over the existing Direct Connect link.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** A full mesh of 60+ VPCs needs n(n-1)/2 peering connections (1,770 for 60 VPCs). Peering is non-transitive, and per-VPC VPNs add even more connections to manage, which is the problem the company already has.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** A transit VPC built on EC2 appliances requires managing instances, licenses, high availability, and throughput limits. Transit Gateway replaces this legacy pattern.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** PrivateLink exposes specific services one way through endpoints. It does not provide general bidirectional routing between VPCs or to on-premises networks, and per-service endpoints across 60 VPCs would be hard to manage.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Many VPCs + on-premises + transitive routing:** AWS Transit Gateway (plus Direct Connect gateway or VPN attachments).\n2. **A few VPCs, simple point-to-point:** VPC peering is acceptable but non-transitive.\n3. **Expose a single service privately:** AWS PrivateLink."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "Gateway Load Balancer",
        "Amazon VPC"
      ],
      "prompt": "A financial services company must inspect all traffic entering and leaving its application VPCs by using a fleet of third-party next-generation firewall virtual appliances from its existing vendor. The appliance fleet must scale horizontally, be highly available, and be inserted transparently into the traffic path without changing source or destination IP addresses. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Place the appliances behind a Network Load Balancer and update the VPC route tables to send traffic to the NLB.",
        "B": "Deploy the appliances behind a Gateway Load Balancer and route traffic to Gateway Load Balancer endpoints in the application VPCs.",
        "C": "Replace the appliances with AWS Network Firewall and route traffic through firewall endpoints.",
        "D": "Place the appliances behind an Application Load Balancer and configure path-based routing rules to each appliance."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Built for virtual appliances:** Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) deploys, scales, and manages third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls, IDS/IPS, and deep packet inspection systems.\n* **Transparent bump-in-the-wire:** GWLB operates at layer 3 and uses GENEVE encapsulation to send the original packets to the appliances, preserving the source and destination IP addresses, while keeping flows sticky to one appliance.\n* **Endpoint integration:** Gateway Load Balancer endpoints (powered by PrivateLink) are used as route table targets, so traffic can be steered through the appliance fleet from any VPC.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** An NLB is designed for client-to-server TCP/UDP load balancing. It does not provide GENEVE encapsulation or the transparent, flow-symmetric bump-in-the-wire insertion that inline inspection appliances need, and it cannot be used as a VPC route table target.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** AWS Network Firewall is a managed firewall, but the requirement is to use the existing vendor's third-party appliances.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** An ALB terminates HTTP/HTTPS connections at layer 7 and cannot transparently forward all IP traffic to inspection appliances.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Third-party virtual appliances / transparent inspection:** Gateway Load Balancer (GENEVE, port 6081).\n2. **Load balancer selection:** ALB = HTTP/HTTPS layer 7 routing; NLB = TCP/UDP/TLS, static IPs, extreme performance; GWLB = appliance insertion."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Data Firehose",
        "AWS Glue Data Catalog",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company collects JSON clickstream events from its web servers at a rate of several thousand events per second. Analysts query the data in Amazon S3 by using Amazon Athena and need new data to be available within a few minutes. The data must be stored in Apache Parquet format to reduce query cost. The company wants a fully managed solution with the LEAST operational overhead. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Write events to an Amazon SQS queue and use an AWS Lambda function to batch them into CSV files in Amazon S3.",
        "B": "Write events directly to Amazon S3 as JSON objects and run a nightly AWS Glue ETL job to convert them to Parquet.",
        "C": "Send events to an Amazon Data Firehose stream with record format conversion enabled that uses an AWS Glue Data Catalog table schema, and deliver the output to Amazon S3.",
        "D": "Stream events to an Amazon EMR cluster that runs Apache Spark Streaming and writes Parquet files to Amazon S3."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Managed near-real-time delivery:** Amazon Data Firehose ingests streaming records, buffers them by size or time, and delivers them to S3 within minutes with no servers to manage.\n* **Built-in format conversion:** Firehose can convert incoming JSON to Apache Parquet or ORC by using a schema from an AWS Glue Data Catalog table, producing columnar files that Athena can query efficiently.\n* **Scales automatically:** Firehose scales with the incoming data rate without capacity planning.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** This requires custom code to batch and write files, and CSV is a row format, so it does not meet the Parquet requirement.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** A nightly job means converted data is up to a day old, which does not meet the few-minutes freshness requirement.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** EMR can perform this transformation, but running and scaling a Spark Streaming cluster has much higher operational overhead than a fully managed Firehose stream.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Streaming data to S3/Redshift/OpenSearch with minimal ops:** Amazon Data Firehose.\n2. **JSON to Parquet/ORC in flight:** Firehose record format conversion with a Glue Data Catalog schema.\n3. **Custom real-time processing with replay and multiple consumers:** Kinesis Data Streams."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "AWS DataSync",
        "AWS Direct Connect",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company must migrate 500 TB of files from an on-premises NFS file server to Amazon S3 within 3 weeks. The data center has a 10 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection with available bandwidth. Users will continue to modify files during the migration, so changed files must be copied incrementally until cutover. The company requires automatic data integrity verification and wants to avoid writing custom scripts. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Order multiple AWS Snowball Edge devices, copy the data to the devices, and ship them to AWS.",
        "B": "Deploy an Amazon S3 File Gateway on premises and have users write files to the gateway's NFS share.",
        "C": "Run the AWS CLI aws s3 sync command from on-premises servers on a schedule.",
        "D": "Deploy an AWS DataSync agent on premises and create scheduled DataSync tasks that transfer data from the NFS share to the S3 bucket over Direct Connect."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Purpose-built online transfer:** DataSync moves data between on-premises NFS/SMB/HDFS storage and AWS storage services, using parallelism and a purpose-built protocol to make full use of available network bandwidth.\n* **Incremental and verified:** Scheduled task executions copy only new or changed data, and DataSync verifies data integrity during and after transfer.\n* **Fits the timeline:** 500 TB over a 10 Gbps link takes roughly 5 days at full utilization, comfortably within 3 weeks.\n* **Managed:** No custom scripts; tasks, scheduling, filtering, and bandwidth throttling are configured in the service.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Offline devices are useful when network bandwidth is insufficient, which is not the case here. They also capture a single point-in-time copy and do not handle ongoing incremental changes until cutover.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** S3 File Gateway provides ongoing hybrid access through a local cache. It is not a bulk migration tool for existing data and does not copy the existing 500 TB from the current NFS server by itself.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Scripted aws s3 sync jobs require custom scheduling, error handling, and monitoring, and do not provide DataSync's managed integrity verification and transfer optimization.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Online migration of NFS/SMB data with incremental sync and verification:** AWS DataSync.\n2. **Limited or no bandwidth, very large datasets:** Offline transfer with the AWS Snow Family.\n3. **Ongoing on-premises access to cloud storage with local caching:** AWS Storage Gateway."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Athena",
        "AWS Glue",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company stores 30 TB of application logs in Amazon S3 as uncompressed CSV files under a single prefix. Analysts use Amazon Athena to run queries that usually filter on a date range and select only a few of the 80 columns. Queries are slow and expensive because Athena scans large amounts of data. Which combination of actions will MOST improve query performance and reduce the amount of data scanned? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Move the log files to the S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class.",
        "B": "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket that stores the logs.",
        "C": "Use an AWS Glue ETL job to convert the CSV files to Apache Parquet format.",
        "D": "Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and query only the latest object versions.",
        "E": "Partition the data in S3 by date and register the partitions in the AWS Glue Data Catalog."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C",
        "E"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options C & E are Correct\n\n* **Columnar format:** Parquet stores data by column and is compressed, so Athena reads only the few columns referenced in the query instead of entire rows, greatly reducing data scanned and cost.\n* **Partitioning:** Partitioning by date (for example, year/month/day prefixes) lets Athena prune partitions and scan only the objects for the requested date range.\n* **Glue integration:** Glue ETL jobs can convert CSV to Parquet and write partitioned output, and the Glue Data Catalog stores the table and partition metadata Athena uses.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Changing the storage class affects storage cost and access fees, not how much data Athena scans. It would increase retrieval costs for frequently queried data.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers to and from S3 over the internet. Athena reads data inside AWS, so this has no effect on query performance.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Versioning keeps previous object versions for recovery. Athena already reads current objects, so versioning does not reduce data scanned.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Athena slow or expensive:** Convert to columnar formats (Parquet/ORC), compress, and partition the data.\n2. **Transforming between formats (CSV to Parquet):** AWS Glue ETL jobs or Data Firehose record format conversion for streams."
    }
  ],
  "set2": [
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon S3 Express One Zone",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs machine learning training jobs on Amazon EC2 instances in a single Availability Zone. Each training run reads millions of small objects through the Amazon S3 API repeatedly, and request latency to the current S3 Standard bucket is now the main bottleneck. The training data can be regenerated from a source bucket if lost. The company needs consistent single-digit millisecond request latency and very high request rates. Which storage solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Move the training data to the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class.",
        "B": "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the existing bucket.",
        "C": "Copy the training data to an S3 directory bucket that uses the S3 Express One Zone storage class in the same Availability Zone as the EC2 instances.",
        "D": "Move the training data to the S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Purpose-built for latency:** S3 Express One Zone is a high-performance storage class that delivers consistent single-digit millisecond data access, up to 10x faster than S3 Standard, and supports hundreds of thousands of requests per second per directory bucket.\n* **Co-location:** Directory buckets are created in a specific Availability Zone, so placing compute in the same AZ minimizes latency.\n* **Acceptable trade-off:** Data is stored redundantly within a single AZ, which is acceptable because the data can be regenerated from the source bucket.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Standard-IA lowers storage cost for infrequently accessed data and adds per-GB retrieval charges. It does not lower latency and is a poor fit for data read repeatedly.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Transfer Acceleration improves long-distance internet transfers by using edge locations. The EC2 instances are already in AWS, so it does not reduce request latency.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** One Zone-IA reduces cost by storing data in one AZ, but it has the same latency profile as other general-purpose S3 storage classes plus retrieval fees.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Single-digit millisecond S3 access for ML/analytics in one AZ:** S3 Express One Zone (directory buckets).\n2. **IA and Glacier classes:** Optimize cost for infrequent access, never performance."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Storage Gateway",
        "Amazon S3 File Gateway",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A manufacturing company wants to store engineering files in Amazon S3 so that cloud-based analytics can use them. On-premises applications must continue to access the same files through an NFS mount and need low-latency access to recently used files. The company's on-premises storage capacity is limited and cannot hold the full dataset. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Use AWS DataSync to copy the files from on premises to Amazon S3 every night.",
        "B": "Deploy Amazon FSx for Windows File Server in AWS and map SMB shares from the on-premises servers over AWS Direct Connect.",
        "C": "Create an Amazon EFS file system and mount it from the on-premises servers over an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection.",
        "D": "Deploy an Amazon S3 File Gateway on premises and mount its NFS file share on the applications."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Hybrid file access to S3:** S3 File Gateway presents an NFS (or SMB) file share on premises and stores each file as a native object in S3, so cloud analytics services can use the objects directly.\n* **Local cache:** The gateway keeps recently used data in a local cache, giving low-latency access without storing the full dataset on premises.\n* **Same data, two access paths:** On-premises apps use file protocols while cloud workloads use the S3 API.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Nightly DataSync copies would require the full dataset to remain on limited on-premises storage, and files in S3 would lag by up to a day.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** FSx for Windows File Server stores data in its own file system, not as S3 objects, uses SMB rather than NFS, and provides no local on-premises cache.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** EFS stores data as a file system, not as S3 objects. Mounting over VPN adds WAN latency for every access because there is no local cache.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **On-premises file access + data in S3 + local cache:** Amazon S3 File Gateway.\n2. **Hybrid storage family:** S3 File Gateway (files to S3), FSx File Gateway (cache for FSx for Windows), Volume Gateway (iSCSI block), Tape Gateway (virtual tape library)."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EFS",
        "AWS Fargate",
        "Amazon ECS"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a Linux-based content management application as Amazon ECS tasks on AWS Fargate across three Availability Zones. All tasks must read and write the same set of uploaded files by using standard file system semantics. The amount of stored data is unpredictable, and the company does not want to provision or manage storage capacity. Which storage solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an Amazon EFS file system with mount targets in each Availability Zone and configure it as a volume in the ECS task definition.",
        "B": "Attach an Amazon EBS io2 volume with Multi-Attach enabled to all of the tasks.",
        "C": "Increase the Fargate ephemeral storage for each task to 200 GiB.",
        "D": "Create an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system and mount it in each task."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Shared, elastic file storage:** EFS is a fully managed NFS file system that grows and shrinks automatically as files are added and removed, so there is no capacity to provision.\n* **Multi-AZ access:** With mount targets in each AZ, tasks in all three AZs can read and write the same files concurrently with POSIX semantics.\n* **Native Fargate integration:** ECS task definitions support EFS volumes for Fargate tasks.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** EBS Multi-Attach is limited to io1/io2 volumes on Nitro EC2 instances within a single AZ and needs a cluster-aware file system. It cannot be shared across Fargate tasks in three AZs.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Fargate ephemeral storage is private to each task and is lost when the task stops, so files are not shared or persistent.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** FSx for Windows File Server provides SMB shares for Windows workloads and is not supported as a volume for Linux tasks on Fargate. It also requires provisioning storage capacity.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Shared Linux file storage across AZs for containers or EC2:** Amazon EFS.\n2. **Windows SMB shares with Active Directory:** Amazon FSx for Windows File Server.\n3. **Per-task scratch space:** Fargate ephemeral storage."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon SNS",
        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company's order service currently calls an inventory service, a shipping service, and an analytics service synchronously for every new order. When any downstream service is slow, order placement fails. Each downstream service must receive every order event, process it at its own pace, and scale independently. Which combination of steps will decouple the architecture? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Have the order service publish each order event to an Amazon SNS topic.",
        "B": "Create a single Amazon SQS standard queue that all three downstream services poll for order events.",
        "C": "Create a separate Amazon SQS queue for each downstream service and subscribe each queue to the SNS topic.",
        "D": "Move the order service to a larger Amazon EC2 instance type to absorb slow downstream responses.",
        "E": "Have the order service invoke each downstream service's AWS Lambda function synchronously."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A",
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options A & C are Correct\n\n* **Publish/subscribe fan-out:** Publishing to an SNS topic lets the order service send each event once without knowing about or waiting for its consumers.\n* **Durable per-consumer buffers:** Each SQS queue subscribed to the topic receives its own copy of every message, buffers it if the service is slow or unavailable, and lets each service scale its consumers independently based on its own queue depth.\n* **Failure isolation:** A slow analytics service no longer affects order placement or the other services.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** With a single queue, each message is delivered to and deleted by one consumer, so the services would compete for messages and not every service would receive every order.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** A larger instance does not remove the synchronous dependency; slow downstream services would still cause failures.\n* **Option E is incorrect:** Synchronous invocation keeps the tight coupling: the order service still waits on each function and fails when one is slow or throttled.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **One event to many independent consumers:** SNS-to-SQS fan-out (or EventBridge with SQS targets).\n2. **Decouple producer and consumer, absorb spikes:** Amazon SQS.\n3. **Competing consumers:** A single queue distributes each message to only one consumer."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Lambda",
        "Lambda SnapStart"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a Java 21 Spring Boot application on AWS Lambda behind Amazon API Gateway. Traffic is unpredictable, and users experience multi-second delays on requests that trigger new execution environments because of the framework's initialization time. The company wants to reduce this startup latency MOST cost-effectively without changing the application code. What should a solutions architect do?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Configure provisioned concurrency sized for peak traffic on the function alias.",
        "B": "Enable Lambda SnapStart on the function and invoke a published version through an alias.",
        "C": "Configure reserved concurrency for the function equal to the expected peak concurrency.",
        "D": "Increase the function timeout to the maximum of 15 minutes."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Snapshot-based startup:** SnapStart initializes the function when a version is published, takes a snapshot of the initialized execution environment, and resumes new environments from the cached snapshot, cutting Java startup latency from seconds to sub-second.\n* **Cost:** For Java runtimes, SnapStart has no additional charge, unlike provisioned concurrency, which bills for pre-initialized environments whether or not they are used.\n* **No code changes:** SnapStart is a configuration setting on published versions; runtime hooks are optional.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Provisioned concurrency also removes cold starts, but sizing it for unpredictable peak traffic incurs continuous charges, so it is not the most cost-effective option.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Reserved concurrency guarantees and caps concurrency. It does not pre-initialize environments or reduce initialization time.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** A longer timeout does not affect initialization latency.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Java (also Python, .NET) Lambda cold starts:** Lambda SnapStart.\n2. **Guaranteed warm environments for predictable traffic:** Provisioned concurrency (can be scheduled with Application Auto Scaling)."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EKS",
        "AWS Fargate"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company is migrating dozens of containerized microservices from an on-premises Kubernetes cluster. The development team wants to keep using its existing Kubernetes manifests and Helm charts. The operations team does not want to provision, patch, or scale any worker nodes, and each pod needs its own isolated compute environment. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy the microservices to Amazon ECS on Amazon EC2 instances managed by an Auto Scaling group.",
        "B": "Deploy the microservices to an Amazon EKS cluster that uses self-managed node groups.",
        "C": "Deploy each microservice as an AWS Elastic Beanstalk Docker environment.",
        "D": "Deploy the microservices to an Amazon EKS cluster and run the pods on AWS Fargate by using Fargate profiles."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Kubernetes compatibility:** Amazon EKS runs upstream-conformant Kubernetes, so existing manifests and Helm charts work without conversion.\n* **Serverless compute for pods:** With Fargate profiles, EKS runs matching pods on Fargate, so there are no worker nodes to provision, patch, or scale.\n* **Isolation:** Each Fargate pod runs in its own isolated compute environment that does not share the kernel, CPU, memory, or network interface with other pods.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Amazon ECS uses its own task definitions rather than Kubernetes manifests, and EC2 instances still need to be managed.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Self-managed node groups require the operations team to provision, patch, and scale the EC2 worker nodes.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Elastic Beanstalk does not use Kubernetes manifests or Helm charts, and it runs containers on EC2 instances that share hosts.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Kubernetes + no node management:** Amazon EKS with AWS Fargate (or EKS Auto Mode).\n2. **AWS-native container orchestration without Kubernetes:** Amazon ECS, with Fargate for serverless compute."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Aurora Serverless v2",
        "Amazon Aurora"
      ],
      "prompt": "A software company is launching a new MySQL-compatible SaaS application. Database load is unpredictable: it is near idle most of the night and spikes to 20 times the baseline within minutes during the day. The company wants the database compute capacity to adjust automatically in fine-grained increments without manual instance resizing or connection disruption. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy an Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible cluster that uses Aurora Serverless v2 instances with minimum and maximum capacity settings.",
        "B": "Deploy Amazon RDS for MySQL and enable storage autoscaling.",
        "C": "Migrate the data model to Amazon DynamoDB with on-demand capacity mode.",
        "D": "Deploy Amazon RDS for MySQL and use a scheduled AWS Lambda function to modify the DB instance class twice a day."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Fine-grained compute scaling:** Aurora Serverless v2 scales capacity in increments of 0.5 Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs) within seconds, based on load, between configured minimum and maximum values.\n* **No disruption:** Scaling happens in place without failover or dropping connections, unlike changing an instance class.\n* **MySQL-compatible:** The application keeps using MySQL-compatible SQL and drivers, and Serverless v2 supports Multi-AZ and Aurora Replicas.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Storage autoscaling increases allocated storage when free space runs low. It does not scale compute capacity for CPU or memory spikes.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value and document database. Moving to it requires redesigning the relational data model and application code, so it is not MySQL-compatible.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Modifying the instance class causes downtime or a failover, and a fixed schedule cannot react to unpredictable spikes within minutes.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Unpredictable or intermittent relational workloads:** Aurora Serverless v2.\n2. **Steady, predictable relational workloads:** Provisioned Aurora or RDS instances, possibly with reserved capacity."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Aurora Global Database",
        "Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL"
      ],
      "prompt": "A global logistics company runs its application on Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible in eu-west-1. The company is deploying application stacks in us-east-1 and ap-southeast-1 and needs low-latency local reads in each Region. Replication lag to the other Regions must typically be under 1 second, and the company must be able to promote another Region to accept writes within minutes if eu-west-1 becomes unavailable. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB global tables with replicas in all three Regions.",
        "B": "Add Aurora Replicas in three Availability Zones in eu-west-1 and route all reads to the reader endpoint.",
        "C": "Convert the cluster to an Aurora Global Database with secondary clusters in us-east-1 and ap-southeast-1.",
        "D": "Convert the deployment to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL Multi-AZ DB cluster with two readable standby instances."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Cross-Region storage replication:** Aurora Global Database replicates at the storage layer with dedicated infrastructure, typically with less than 1 second of lag to secondary Regions.\n* **Local reads:** Each secondary cluster serves low-latency reads to the application stack in its Region.\n* **Regional failover:** A secondary cluster can be promoted to a full read/write primary through managed switchover or failover, typically within minutes (RTO measured in minutes).\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** DynamoDB global tables are multi-Region and multi-active, but DynamoDB is not PostgreSQL-compatible. The relational application would need to be redesigned.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Aurora Replicas within eu-west-1 improve read scaling and AZ-level availability only. Users in other Regions would still see cross-Region latency, and there is no Regional failover.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** A Multi-AZ DB cluster provides readable standbys across AZs in a single Region. It does not provide local reads or failover in other Regions.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Relational + multi-Region low-latency reads + sub-second replication + Regional DR:** Aurora Global Database.\n2. **NoSQL multi-Region active-active writes:** DynamoDB global tables."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.3",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon ElastiCache",
        "Amazon RDS for MySQL"
      ],
      "prompt": "An ecommerce company's product catalog pages query an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance. Monitoring shows that the same small set of SELECT queries makes up 90% of database load, and CPU on the DB instance reaches 95% during sales events. Catalog data can be up to 5 minutes stale. Traffic during sales events is spiky, and the company does not want to manage cache nodes or capacity. Which solution will MOST improve performance?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy Amazon ElastiCache Serverless and implement a cache-aside (lazy loading) strategy with a 5-minute TTL for catalog query results.",
        "B": "Enable a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment and send catalog queries to the standby instance.",
        "C": "Place Amazon RDS Proxy in front of the DB instance and connect the catalog pages through the proxy.",
        "D": "Migrate the DB instance storage to Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) with the maximum IOPS supported."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Cache repeated reads:** Serving the frequent, identical query results from an in-memory cache removes most of the read load from the database and returns results in sub-millisecond time.\n* **Staleness tolerance:** A TTL of 5 minutes on cached items keeps data within the allowed staleness while cache-aside populates the cache only with data that is actually requested.\n* **Serverless operation:** ElastiCache Serverless automatically scales cache capacity with traffic, so there are no nodes or shards to size for sales events.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** The standby in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment is for failover only and cannot serve read traffic.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** RDS Proxy pools and shares connections. It does not cache query results, so the same queries would still consume database CPU.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** The bottleneck is CPU from repeated queries, not storage I/O. Higher IOPS adds cost without reducing the query load.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Repeated reads + staleness tolerated + reduce DB load:** Amazon ElastiCache (Valkey, Redis OSS, or Memcached) with a TTL.\n2. **Caching strategies:** Lazy loading (cache-aside) caches only requested data but can serve stale data; write-through keeps the cache current but caches data that might never be read.\n3. **Multi-AZ DB instance standby:** Not readable; use read replicas or a Multi-AZ DB cluster for readable standbys."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon CloudFront",
        "Amazon S3",
        "Origin access control (OAC)"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company hosts static assets (images, JavaScript, and CSS) for its website in a private Amazon S3 bucket in us-west-2. Users across North America, Europe, and Asia report slow page loads. The company wants to reduce latency for all users, keep the bucket private, and minimize operational overhead. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and update the website to use the accelerate endpoint for the assets.",
        "B": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the S3 bucket as the origin, use origin access control (OAC), and update the bucket policy to allow access only from the distribution.",
        "C": "Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator with the S3 bucket as an endpoint.",
        "D": "Replicate the bucket to Regions in Europe and Asia with S3 Cross-Region Replication and use Route 53 latency-based routing to direct users to the nearest bucket."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Edge caching:** CloudFront caches static content at hundreds of edge locations worldwide, so most requests are served close to users with low latency and reduced load on the origin.\n* **Private origin:** Origin access control (OAC) lets CloudFront sign requests to S3, and the bucket policy allows only the distribution, so the bucket stays private. OAC is the recommended replacement for legacy origin access identity (OAI).\n* **Low overhead:** A single managed distribution serves all Regions.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Transfer Acceleration optimizes long-distance transfers to S3 but does not cache content at the edge, and it requires public or presigned access to the bucket for website visitors.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Global Accelerator endpoints are Application Load Balancers, Network Load Balancers, EC2 instances, and Elastic IP addresses. An S3 bucket cannot be a Global Accelerator endpoint, and Global Accelerator does not cache content.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Multi-Region replication with latency routing can reduce latency, but it multiplies storage cost and operational overhead, provides no edge caching, and makes it harder to keep the buckets private for direct browser access.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Global users + static content + low latency:** Amazon CloudFront.\n2. **Private S3 origin for CloudFront:** Origin access control (OAC), not legacy OAI.\n3. **Global Accelerator vs. CloudFront:** Global Accelerator for TCP/UDP apps needing static IPs and fast failover; CloudFront for cacheable HTTP(S) content."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "Network Load Balancer",
        "Elastic Load Balancing"
      ],
      "prompt": "A financial trading platform uses a proprietary TCP-based protocol and must handle millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency. Client firms require a static IP address in each Availability Zone so they can configure firewall allowlists, and the backend servers must see the original client IP addresses. Which load balancing solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "An Application Load Balancer with listener rules that forward traffic to target groups of EC2 instances.",
        "B": "A Gateway Load Balancer with the EC2 instances registered as appliances.",
        "C": "A Classic Load Balancer with cross-zone load balancing enabled.",
        "D": "A Network Load Balancer with an Elastic IP address assigned to each Availability Zone subnet mapping."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Layer 4 performance:** Network Load Balancers operate at layer 4 (TCP, UDP, TLS), handle millions of requests per second, and add very low latency, which suits proprietary TCP protocols.\n* **Static IPs:** An NLB provides one static IP per AZ, and you can assign your own Elastic IP address to each subnet mapping for allowlisting.\n* **Client IP preservation:** NLB supports client IP preservation (enabled by default for instance targets and configurable for IP targets), so backends see the original client IP addresses.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** An ALB works at layer 7 for HTTP/HTTPS/gRPC and does not support arbitrary TCP protocols. ALB IP addresses also change and cannot be fixed with Elastic IPs.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Gateway Load Balancer is designed to insert third-party virtual appliances transparently into network paths, not to load balance client application traffic.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Classic Load Balancer is a previous-generation load balancer, does not provide static IP addresses per AZ, and does not offer NLB's performance.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **TCP/UDP + extreme performance + static IP/Elastic IP + source IP preservation:** Network Load Balancer.\n2. **HTTP path/host-based routing, gRPC, authentication:** Application Load Balancer.\n3. **Static IP in front of an ALB:** Put the ALB behind an NLB (ALB-type target group) or use Global Accelerator."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.4",
      "topics": [
        "AWS PrivateLink",
        "Network Load Balancer",
        "Amazon VPC"
      ],
      "prompt": "A SaaS provider runs an API in its own VPC and must make it available to hundreds of customer VPCs in different AWS accounts. Many customer VPCs use CIDR ranges that overlap with each other and with the provider's VPC. Traffic must not traverse the internet, and customers must not gain network access to any other resources in the provider's VPC. Which solution meets these requirements and scales to new customers?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Expose the API through a VPC endpoint service (AWS PrivateLink) backed by a Network Load Balancer, and have each customer create an interface VPC endpoint.",
        "B": "Create a VPC peering connection between the provider VPC and each customer VPC.",
        "C": "Attach the provider VPC and all customer VPCs to a shared AWS Transit Gateway and propagate routes between them.",
        "D": "Establish an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection from each customer VPC to a virtual private gateway on the provider VPC."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Service-level private connectivity:** PrivateLink exposes only the specific service behind the NLB. Consumers access it through elastic network interfaces in their own subnets, with no route to other provider resources.\n* **Overlapping CIDRs:** Because no routes are exchanged between VPCs, overlapping IP address ranges are not a problem.\n* **Scale and privacy:** Traffic stays on the AWS network, and the provider can accept endpoint connections from any number of customer accounts.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** VPC peering cannot be established between VPCs with overlapping CIDR blocks and grants network-level access between the VPCs rather than access to a single service.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Transit Gateway routes between attached VPCs, which fails with overlapping CIDRs and would expose more than the single API.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Site-to-Site VPN over the internet uses encrypted tunnels across public networks, is cumbersome to manage per customer, and still has the overlapping-CIDR routing problem.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Provide a service to many VPCs/accounts privately, overlapping CIDRs:** AWS PrivateLink (endpoint service + NLB, interface endpoints).\n2. **Full network connectivity between many VPCs:** Transit Gateway (non-overlapping CIDRs required)."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"
      ],
      "prompt": "A ride-sharing company ingests vehicle location updates from its mobile apps into an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams data stream in provisioned capacity mode. Traffic has quadrupled and is now unpredictable. Producers receive ProvisionedThroughputExceededException errors, and investigation shows that records use the city region code as the partition key, so a few shards receive most of the traffic. Which combination of actions will resolve the write throttling? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Increase the data retention period of the stream to 7 days.",
        "B": "Switch the stream to on-demand capacity mode.",
        "C": "Register the consumer applications with enhanced fan-out.",
        "D": "Change the partition key to a high-cardinality value such as the vehicle ID.",
        "E": "Increase the buffer interval of the downstream Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B",
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options B & D are Correct\n\n* **Even distribution:** Each shard supports up to 1 MB/s or 1,000 records/s of writes. A low-cardinality partition key creates hot shards; a high-cardinality key such as the vehicle ID spreads records evenly across all shards.\n* **Elastic capacity:** On-demand mode automatically scales the stream's capacity with traffic, which suits unpredictable growth without manual shard management.\n* **Both are needed:** More capacity alone does not fix hot shards, and a better key alone does not add capacity for a 4x increase.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Retention controls how long records are stored for replay. It does not change write throughput.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Enhanced fan-out gives each consumer dedicated read throughput of 2 MB/s per shard. It addresses consumer read contention, not producer write throttling.\n* **Option E is incorrect:** Firehose buffering affects delivery to destinations downstream of the stream and has no effect on producer writes into Kinesis Data Streams.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **ProvisionedThroughputExceededException on writes:** Fix hot partition keys and add shards (or use on-demand mode).\n2. **Slow or contending consumers:** Enhanced fan-out.\n3. **Unpredictable streaming traffic:** Kinesis Data Streams on-demand capacity mode."
    },
    {
      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Lake Formation",
        "Amazon Athena",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company is building a data lake in Amazon S3 that is cataloged in the AWS Glue Data Catalog and queried with Amazon Athena. Different analyst teams must see only specific columns of some tables, and regional teams must see only rows for their own region. The company wants to manage these permissions centrally by using database-style grants instead of maintaining complex S3 bucket policies. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create separate S3 prefixes for each team and grant access by using S3 bucket policies and IAM policies.",
        "B": "Create Athena workgroups for each team and restrict each workgroup's query result location.",
        "C": "Register the S3 locations with AWS Lake Formation and grant column-level and row-level permissions by using Lake Formation data filters.",
        "D": "Use Amazon Macie to classify the data and block analysts from querying sensitive columns."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Centralized fine-grained access:** Lake Formation provides a central permissions model (GRANT/REVOKE) for Data Catalog databases and tables, including column-level, row-level, and cell-level security through data filters.\n* **Integrated enforcement:** Athena, Amazon EMR, AWS Glue, and Amazon Redshift Spectrum enforce Lake Formation permissions when querying registered S3 locations.\n* **Simpler governance:** Permissions can also be managed at scale with LF-Tags instead of per-object policies.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** S3 bucket and IAM policies control access to whole objects or prefixes. They cannot restrict access to specific columns or rows inside a file.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Workgroups separate query execution, cost controls, and result locations. They do not restrict which columns or rows a user can query.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Macie discovers and classifies sensitive data in S3. It does not enforce query-time permissions in Athena.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Data lake + fine-grained (column/row/cell) permissions:** AWS Lake Formation.\n2. **Discover sensitive data (PII) in S3:** Amazon Macie.\n3. **Serverless SQL on S3:** Amazon Athena with the Glue Data Catalog."
    },
