      "domain": 3,
      "task": "3.5",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon MSK",
        "Amazon MSK Serverless",
        "Apache Kafka"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs dozens of on-premises producer and consumer applications that use the Apache Kafka client APIs to stream order events. The company is moving the workloads to AWS and wants to avoid modifying application code. The operations team does not want to provision brokers, manage storage, or plan cluster capacity as throughput changes. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Migrate the applications to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in on-demand capacity mode.",
        "B": "Create an Amazon MSK Serverless cluster and point the applications to its bootstrap brokers.",
        "C": "Deploy a self-managed Apache Kafka cluster on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group.",
        "D": "Create an Amazon MQ for RabbitMQ broker and connect the applications to it."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Kafka compatibility:** Amazon MSK runs open-source Apache Kafka, so existing producers and consumers work by changing only the bootstrap broker endpoint (and authentication settings).\n* **Serverless capacity:** MSK Serverless automatically provisions and scales compute and storage, so there are no brokers to size or storage to manage.\n* **Managed operations:** AWS handles patching, availability, and scaling of the Kafka infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Kinesis Data Streams uses a different API from Kafka, so every producer and consumer would have to be rewritten.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Self-managed Kafka on EC2 keeps all broker provisioning, patching, storage, and capacity planning with the operations team.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Amazon MQ supports ActiveMQ and RabbitMQ protocols such as AMQP and MQTT, not the Kafka API, so the applications would require code changes.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Existing Kafka apps + managed:** Amazon MSK (provisioned) or MSK Serverless (no capacity management).\n2. **Existing JMS/AMQP/MQTT apps:** Amazon MQ.\n3. **AWS-native streaming with no Kafka dependency:** Kinesis Data Streams."
    }
  ]
